## Course Overview

This course introduces the **computational foundations of modern statistics** and demonstrates how statistical reasoning is transformed into algorithms that operate on real data. Rather than focusing solely on closed-form derivations, the course emphasizes **simulation, approximation, and algorithmic inference**, reflecting how statistics is actually practiced in contemporary data science.

Students will begin by revisiting probability and statistical inference from a computational perspective, exploring how randomness, sampling, and uncertainty are represented and manipulated in code. The curriculum then advances through **Monte Carlo simulation**, **law of large numbers and central limit phenomena via computation**, and **random number generation**, forming the backbone of simulation-based statistics.

Key inferential techniques such as **maximum likelihood estimation**, **method of moments**, and **Bayesian inference** will be developed alongside their numerical implementations, highlighting optimization–statistics connections. The course places strong emphasis on **resampling methods**, including bootstrap and permutation tests, as powerful tools for uncertainty quantification when analytical formulas are unavailable or unreliable.

Further topics include **hypothesis testing in high dimensions**, **multiple testing corrections**, **nonparametric methods**, and **kernel density estimation**, all framed through computational efficiency and scalability. Regression and generalized linear models are revisited from a computational standpoint, incorporating numerical stability, regularization, and model diagnostics.

Advanced sections may introduce **Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC)**, **Gibbs sampling**, **Metropolis–Hastings**, and **approximate Bayesian computation**, illustrating how modern Bayesian workflows are constructed in practice. Throughout the course, students will learn how statistical algorithms integrate into data science workflows such as exploratory data analysis, feature engineering, and model evaluation.

